WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:07.760
Normally when you pip install a Python package, it&nbsp;
will download from PyPI, the Python package index.&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.200
But did you know that you can actually&nbsp;
pip install directly from GitHub?&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.080
Take the git URL to your favorite&nbsp;
project and add "git+" in front.&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.280
Then you can just pip install it to try&nbsp;
out the latest commit from that repo.&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.600
You can also add an "@" sign with a git reference,&nbsp;
such a branch, tag name, commit hash, or pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.240
request number to install a specific version.
This can be useful for testing versions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.040
packages that haven't been officially published&nbsp;
yet, like from a feature branch or a pull request.&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.800
It's good practice to also specify the&nbsp;
egg name, which is what pip will treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.200
this project's name as for the purposes of&nbsp;
dependency checking, so if you later install&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.440
another package that depends on the GitHub&nbsp;
one it will know it's already installed.&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.000
All this only works if the repo is an&nbsp;
actual Python package with a setup.py file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.880
not just a lonely script.
Check out my video on automated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.520
testing if you want to see how to set that up.
If the setup.py isn't at the root of the repo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.200
you can specify it with the&nbsp;
subdirectory query parameter in the URL.

